==English==

===Etymology===
* First attested around 1325.
* From {{inh|en|enm|abounden}}, {{m|enm|abounde}}, from {{der|en|fro|abonder}}, {{m|fro|abunder}}, from {{der|en|la|abundāre}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|abundō||overflow}}, which comes from {{m|la|ab||from, down from}} + {{m|la|undō||surge, swell, rise in waves, move in waves}}, from {{m|la|unda||wave}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbaʊnd/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbaʊnd/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abound.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊnd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be full to [[overflowing]]. {{defdate|First attested from around (1350 to 1470).}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=7}}</ref>
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To be [[wealthy]]. {{defdate|Attested from around (1350 to 1470) until the mid 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be highly [[productive]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be present or available in large numbers or quantities; to be plentiful. {{defdate|First attested from around (1350 to 1470).}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{ux|en|Wild animals '''abound''' wherever man does not stake his claim.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Romans|5|20|passage=Moreouer, the Lawe entred, that the offence might '''abound''': but where sinne '''abounded''', grace did much more '''abound'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=December|title=New G.E. Line diesel loco maintenance depot at Stratford|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=766|text=One end of the east-west building is wet, the other windy, and at present there is smoke '''abounding''', too; but these distressing yard elements can be completely excluded at each end by full-width folding doors [...].}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To revel in. {{defdate|Attested from around (1350 to 1470) until the late 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{senseid|en|be copiously supplied with}} To be [[copiously]] supplied. {{+obj|en|:in/:with<something>}}
#: {{ux|en|The wilderness '''abounds''' in traps.}}
#: {{ux|en|This pond '''abounds''' with fish.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=III|page=108|passage=I could plainly diſcover from whence one Family derives a long Chin; why a ſecond hath '''abounded''' with Knaves for two Generations, and Fools for two more; why a third happened to be crack-brained, and a fourth to be Sharpers.}}
#* '''1858-1860''', {{w|George Rawlinson}}, ''The Five Great Monarchies of the Ancient Eastern World''
#*: the wild boar, which '''abounds''' both in Azerbijan and in the country about Hamadan

====Usage notes====
* This is generally a [[stative verb]] that rarely takes the continuous inflection. See [[:Category:English stative verbs]]

